                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon_j y
            \. So this makes the cubical theory nearer to the simplicial. Cubical complexes with this, and other, structures have also been considered by Evrard.
          %p
            The first appearance of this notion in dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            was in the paper by Brown and Spencer listed below, and used to obtain an equivalence between crossed modules and edge symmetric double groupoids with connection.
          %p
            Such connections on cubical sets were introduced in 1981 by Brown and Higgins in order to obtain the equivalence of their “cubical
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complexes"} crossed complexes
            = succeed "-shell\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h2#definition_as_presheaves_on_strict_test_categories Definition as presheaves on strict test categories
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              The category of cubes with symmetric connections is defined as follows. We start with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              whose objects are finite
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  ordered sets
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/ordered+sets"} ?
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,\le)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,\le)\to(T,\le)
              are maps of sets
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mi S
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}^S\to\{0,1\}^T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,\le)\to(\emptyset,\le)
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\emptyset,\le)\to(S,\le)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,\le)\to(1,\le)
              that compute the maximum or minimum of an
              = succeed "-tuple" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              The category of cubical sets with symmetric or asymmetric connections is the category of
              %span.newWikiWord
                presheaves of sets
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/presheaves+of+sets"} ?
              on the category of cubes with symmetric or asymmetric connections.
          %p It would seem that…
          %p
            Alternatively, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo ⊂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_{\subset}
            be the category whose objects are
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            the functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi m
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}m \to \mathcal{P}n
            preserving
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊆
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \subseteq
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} box
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo ⊂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J : \box \to \mathcal{P}_{\subset}
            such that restriction of presheaves along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            is exactly “forget connection structure”.
          %p
            There is also a faithful representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo ⊂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_{\subset}
            in polyhedra-with-boundary and piecewise-linear maps, valued on objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\mapsto [0,1]^n
            with the coordinate-ordered simplicial subdivision of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]^n
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#as_a_model_for_homotopy_theory As a model for homotopy theory
          %p
            The ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cube+category"} cube category
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/test+category"} test category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+sets"} cubical sets
            carry the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            is that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
          %p
            But the category of cubes
            %em with connection
            is even a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+test+category"} strict test category
            (
            %a{:href => "#Maltsiniotis"}> Maltsiniotis, 2008
            ). This means that under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
            (see the discussion at
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            of cubical sets with connection is sent to the correct product
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
          %p
            The lack of this property for cubical sets without connections was one of the original reasons for abandoning Kan’s initial cubical approach to combinatorial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            in favour of the simplicial approach; the implications of this new result have yet to be thought through. Another reason was that cubical groups were in general not Kan complexes; however cubical groups with connection are Kan complexes. See the paper by Tonks listed below.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            The prime example of a cubical set with connections is the singular cubical complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KX
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  n
                  \\ge 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_n
            is the set of singular
            = succeed "-cubes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (i.e. continuous maps
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^n   \to   X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi i
                    %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Gamma_i^\alpha :K_{n } \to   K_{n+1}
            is induced by the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi γ
                    %mi i
                    %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\gamma_i^\alpha   : I^{n+1} \to
                  I^{n}
            defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi γ
                    %mi i
                    %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\gamma _i^\alpha (t_1 ,t_2 ,\ldots \, ,t_{n+1} ) =
                  (t_1 ,t_2 ,\ldots\, ,t_{i-1},A(t_i ,t_{i+1}),t_{i+2},\ldots \, ,t_{n+1} )
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi max
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi min
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(s,t)=\max(s,t), \min(s,t)
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha=-,+
            respectively.
          %p
            The first hint of such a general structure came in the paper by Brown and Spencer given below. The term “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection"}> connection
            ” was used there because of a relation of a generalisation of this idea to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} path-connections
            in differential geometry. A principal
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            gives rise to the Ehresmann groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Equ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Equ(E)
            of
            = succeed "-maps" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            on this form a double category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Equ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Equ(E)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda(B)
            as edge categories. A connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            is then a functor from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda(B)
            to one of the category structures on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            which gives a smooth lifting of paths to transport of the fibres. This is the origin of the term
            %span.newWikiWord
              transport law
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/transport+law"} ?
            for the relation of connections to composition.
          %p
            %strong Discussion
          %p
            There is a discussion of cubical vs simplicial singular homology and for other aspects at (
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/3656/cubical-vs-simplicial-singular-homology"}> this mathoverflow
            ).
          %p
            A complete cubical approach to algebraic topology at the border between homotopy and homology is given in the book discussed in these pages at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nonabelian+Algebraic+Topology"} Nonabelian Algebraic Topology
            see (
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Nonabelian+Algebraic+Topology"}> here
            ). As an example, the theory shows how the
            %span.newWikiWord
              Relative Hurewicz Theorem
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Relative+Hurewicz+Theorem"} ?
            follows from a Higher Homotopy Seifert-van Kampen Theorem, withut using singular homology.
          %h2#model_structure Model structure
          %p
            The category of cubical sets with connections is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+test+category"} strict test category
            and therefore admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+model+structure"} cartesian model structure
            that is Quillen equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan%E2%80%93Quillen+model+structure"} Kan–Quillen model structure
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Maltsiniotis"} Maltsiniotis
          %p
            See also the article
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cubical+sets"} model structure on cubical sets
          %h2#joyaltype_model_structures Joyal-type model structures
          %p
            In complete analogy to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joyal+model+structure"} Joyal model structure
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+sets"} cubical sets
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures+for+cubical+quasicategories"} model structures for cubical quasicategories
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical set
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cubical+sets"} model structure on cubical sets
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures+for+cubical+quasicategories"} model structures for cubical quasicategories
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"} Ronnie Brown
                and C.B. Spencer, “Double groupoids and crossed modules’’, Cah. Top. Géom. Diff. 17 (1976) 343–362.
            %li
              %p Evrard, M., “Homotopie des complexes simpliciaux et cubiques”, Preprint(1976).
            %li
              %p
                Brown, R. and Higgins, P.J., “On the algebra of cubes”,
                %em J. Pure Appl. Algebra
                21 (1981) 233–260.
            %li
              %p
                F. Al-Agl, R. Brown and R. Steiner, “Multiple categories: the equivalence between a globular and cubical approach”,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Advances in Mathematics
            %li
              %p
                M. Grandis and L. Mauri, “Cubical sets and their site”,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Theory Applic. Categories
            %li
              %p
                P.J. Higgins, “Thin elements and commutative shells in cubical
                = succeed "-categories\u201D," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                %em Theory Appl. Categ.
                14 (2005) 60–74.
          %p The statement that cubical groups with connections are Kan complexes is due to
          %ul
            %li
              A. Tonks, “Cubical groups which are Kan”,
              = succeed "," do
                %em J. Pure Appl. Algebra
          %p Cubical sets with connection are used in the following paper:
          %ul
            %li I. Patchkoria “Cubical approach to derived functors” Homology Homotopy Appl. Volume 14, Number 1 (2012), 133-158.
          %p and in the following in preference to simplicial methods to take resolutions in an analytic motivic setting:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                J. Ayoub - “L’algèbre de Hopf et le groupe de Galois motiviques d’un corps de caractéristique nulle, I”
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://user.math.uzh.ch/ayoub/PDF-Files/GaloisMotivic-1.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p A Vezzani - “A motivic version of the theorem of Fontaine and Wintenberger” arXiv:1405.4548,
          %p
            The statement that cubes with max-connections form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+test+category"} strict test category
            is due to
          %ul#Maltsiniotis
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"}> Georges Maltsiniotis
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em La catégorie cubique avec connections est une catégorie test stricte
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.intlpress.com/HHA/v11/n2/a15/"} web
          %p based on
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Les préfaisceaux comme modeèle des types d’homotopie
          %p The case of cubes with both max-connections and min-connections is analogous and was treated explicitly in Corollary 3 and Theorem 3 of
          %ul
            %li
              Ulrik Buchholtz, Edward Morehouse,
